  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.3. Increased institutional capacity of labour administrations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In December 2020, the Government of Brazil launched an online tool (Ip&#xea;) that allows the expansion of the knowledge base, in real time, about victims and perpetrators of forced labour, increasing the labour administration&#xb4;s capacity to be better informed during the planning of labour inspection actions. Ip&#xea; consists of a platform easily accessed through internet or a mobile device (iOS and Android systems). It receives, systematizes, and forwards complaints in relation to exploitative situations to the labour inspectorate, who can then expedite the decision-making process and the monitoring of the reported cases. With the objective of reaching migrant victims from other countries, the system allows the filing of allegations in Spanish, English and French, apart from Portuguese. Ip&#xea; also counts with a module that allows for a deeper socioeconomic, demographic, and labour characterization of the rescued workers and, allowing the planning of public policies and actions on prevention and reinsertion of victims. This system resulted in a more effective and efficient labour inspection system regarding unacceptable forms of work.
 The ILO conducted technical meetings with the government, especially the Ministry of Economy, employers&#xb4; and workers&#xb4; organizations in order to gather technical inputs  to design both online tools. The ILO served as an honest broker in facilitating the dialogue between the Labour Prosecutor&#xb4;s Office (MPT) and the Ministry of Economy in order to allow the exchange of data between the two institutions who are the main authorities for conducting labour violations inspections and guaranteeing workers&#xb4; rights.</narrative>
   </description>
